---------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY NOTICE — Remotion is NOT MIT licensed
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MIT license above covers HITE's own source code. It does NOT relicense
HITE's dependencies, and one of them carries terms you must read before using
this project commercially.

HITE's video engine is Remotion (`remotion`, `@remotion/bundler`,
`@remotion/player`, `@remotion/renderer`). Remotion is source-available under
its own license, not an OSI open-source license. In summary, as of this
writing:

  * Free for individuals, for non-profit organisations, and for for-profit
    companies with up to 3 employees.
  * Companies above that threshold need a paid Remotion Company License.

This is not legal advice and the summary may go out of date — read the current
terms at https://www.remotion.dev/docs/license and https://remotion.pro before
deploying HITE commercially.

Remotion is central to how HITE renders video (it drives both the preview and
the export), so it cannot simply be removed. If your organisation cannot accept
Remotion's terms, you cannot run HITE's render path as shipped.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY NOTICE — ffmpeg binaries (GPL) and GSAP
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Two further dependencies carry terms that are not MIT. Neither changes the
licence of HITE's own source, but both bind you when you distribute or deploy.

`ffmpeg-static` and `ffprobe-static` ship prebuilt FFmpeg binaries licensed
GPL-3.0-or-later. HITE invokes them as separate executables over a child
process and never links against them, which is why HITE's own source can stay
MIT. If you redistribute a build that bundles those binaries, the GPL's terms
travel with the binaries: ship the corresponding source offer, or swap in an
FFmpeg you have built under terms you can accept. Nothing in HITE depends on a
particular build; `lib/ffmpeg/bin.ts` is the one place the path is resolved.

`gsap` is used only by the marketing landing page's scroll sequence. It is
distributed under GSAP's own "standard, no charge" licence, not an OSI licence
— free for the great majority of uses, including commercial ones, but with
terms of its own. Read them at https://gsap.com/standard-license. Nothing in
the editor, the planner, the reducer or the render path imports it, so removing
the landing page removes the dependency entirely.

`@axe-core/playwright`, used only by the accessibility checks in `qa/`, is
MPL-2.0. It is a development dependency and is not part of any shipped bundle.

This is not legal advice, and these summaries can go out of date. Read the
current terms before redistributing.
